ArchiveBinsafe: bound the hash table's insertion index before it indexes the entry vector.

`ReadArchiveBinsafe::read_header` reads a BinSafe hash table straight off the
file:

	auto hash_table_size = read->read_uint();
	_m_hash_table_entries.resize(hash_table_size);

	for (std::uint32_t i = 0; i < hash_table_size; ++i) {
		auto key_length = read->read_ushort();
		auto insertion_index = read->read_ushort();
		auto hash_value = read->read_uint();
		auto key = read->read_string(key_length);

		_m_hash_table_entries[insertion_index] = hash_table_entry {key, hash_value};
	}

`_m_hash_table_entries` is sized to `hash_table_size`, but `insertion_index` —
the index actually used to write into it — is a second, independent count read
from the same untrusted file and never checked against that size. A corrupted
`insertion_index` larger than the table is not a bounds-checked failure, it is
`std::vector::operator[]` writing past the end of the backing allocation: an
out-of-bounds heap write, which is a stronger and less predictable failure than
an out-of-bounds *read* — consistent with the mix of `0xC0000005` (access
violation) and `0xC0000374` (heap corruption) seen fuzzing this file (§16.11).

Found by fuzzing `test/fixtures/minimal.g2.zen` (60 bytes flipped at random,
several dozen seeds) and bisecting one `0xC0000005` crash down to a single
byte: file offset 3983, the low byte of the `insertion_index` field belonging
to the `rangeAniSmooth` hash table entry (the table has 55 entries; corrupting
this one field to a value outside `[0, 55)` reproduces the crash).

The fix is a bound on this one field before it is used to index, in the same
narrow style as patch `0027`: check the count the file supplies against what
it is actually allowed to address, and throw a named `ParserError` instead of
trusting it. This is one instance of the unvalidated-count class documented in
§16.11, not the class-level fix — `BspTree::load` indexes its polygon list with
two more unbounded file-supplied values (bounded since, by `0030`), and the
general fix (making `ReadMemory::seek` clamp or throw) is still deferred, so
other chunk-walking loops throughout `Mesh.cc`, `BspTree.cc` and the VOB
readers remain unbounded.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain bounds-check bug, no API change, one guard.

diff --git a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
index 54151fa..4e599e1 100644
--- a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
@@ -29,6 +29,14 @@ namespace zenkit {
 				auto hash_value = read->read_uint();
 				auto key = read->read_string(key_length);
 
+				// `insertion_index` comes straight from the file and indexes a vector sized
+				// only to `hash_table_size` -- a corrupted index past that bound is an
+				// out-of-bounds write into heap memory, not a bounds-checked failure.
+				if (insertion_index >= hash_table_size) {
+					throw ParserError {"ReadArchive.Binsafe",
+					                   "hash table entry names an insertion index outside the table"};
+				}
+
 				_m_hash_table_entries[insertion_index] = hash_table_entry {key, hash_value};
 			}
 
